articles.text:
The ecological budgets fix, for the Republic and for each region and watershed, the ceilings and floors governing:
primary energy supplied, by source;
materials extracted, imported and placed in use, by mass and by category;
greenhouse gas emissions, together with a cumulative budget expressed as a total remaining quantity;
freshwater abstraction and return, computed per basin;
conversion, sealing and degradation of land and soil;
nitrogen, phosphorus and other nutrient flows;
release of toxic and novel substances;
harvest of forests, fisheries and other living populations, set below documented regeneration;
the minimum area and connectivity of habitat held in protection;
the minimum quantity of restoration owed in each period.
Materials and energy embodied in imports are counted against the budgets of the Republic. In no case may a domestic ceiling be discharged by purchase of an offset, credit or entitlement outside the territory.
Budgets are set for periods of five years, with annual ceilings within each period.

Before adopting a budget the Authority collects and examines:
the ecological reports of cooperatives and enterprises on their own operations;
the reports and objections of worker assemblies, consumer assemblies and unions;
the ecological studies and monitoring records of communes and regions;
the knowledge held by inhabitants of the affected territory concerning its condition and history;
independent scientific work submitted by any person or institution.
Failure to furnish a report required under paragraph 1(a) is an offence and grounds for suspension of the resource allocation of the organisation in default.
The draft budget is published for ninety days. Every substantiated objection receives a written and public answer, and the final budget states the changes made in consequence.
Any person may petition for revision upon new evidence. The Authority answers within ninety days, and its refusal is reviewable under Article 27.
A region or commune may adopt local limits stricter than the budget. Adoption of weaker limits is void.
Revision that raises a ceiling requires published evidence that the higher figure remains within documented regenerative capacity, together with the concurring opinion of the scientific institutions designated by organic law.

The plan is adopted for a period of five years, with annual quantities within it, and follows a cycle of six stages:
registration of needs under Article 63, and the statements of the workplace, user and neighbourhood assemblies under Articles 92 and 93;
computation by the Secretariat of provisional balances, and publication of the deficits, the surpluses and the variants;
rounds of negotiation, in which each level reconciles the offers and needs before it, and refers upward only what it has failed to reconcile;
reconciliation and adoption under Article 98;
execution, with the in-year adjustment of Article 99;
public account of the period, examined by the assemblies that furnished its inputs.
Stage (c) comprises no fewer than three rounds, and a round concludes only after the objections received have been answered in writing and published.
A matter is decided at the lowest level at which the effects of the decision fall, and a higher level takes up a matter only upon referral under paragraph 1(c) or where the effects extend beyond one level.
Every stage is public. A plan adopted upon inputs withheld from publication is void.

Provision is organised in four categories:
essential provision under Article 62, free at the point of use and guaranteed as minimum core;
general provision under Article 107, free at the point of use;
minor discretionary goods, allocated through the residual unit of account under Article 111;
resource-intensive goods, held and used in common under Article 112.
Movement of a good from category (c) to category (b) as production permits is a standing objective of the plan.
Movement of a good from category (b) to category (c) requires a published demonstration of scarcity and is presumed unconstitutional under Article 41(4).
Categories (a) and (b) are furnished free of charge, and in no case is payment, credit, deposit, membership, contribution or proof of anything required for access to them.

The following acts are void, whoever adopts them, by whatever majority and by whatever procedure:
alienation, transfer, pledge or grant in exclusive right of a commons resource, contrary to Articles 9(2) and 83(2);
the reintroduction of capital markets, of tradable claims on productive assets, of enterprises operating for profit, of lending at interest, or of the employment of persons for the appropriation of their surplus labour, contrary to Articles 6(4) and 88(3);
the letting for gain of a dwelling, of land, of tools or of a productive asset, contrary to Articles 65(6) and 88(3);
the grant of an exclusive right over knowledge, including a patent, contrary to Article 10(2);
the abolition of essential provision, its reduction below the minimum core, or its conditioning upon contribution, conduct, status, document, debt or behaviour, contrary to Article 62(2) and (3);
the introduction of a charge for a good or service falling within Articles 62 and 107;
concentration of the means of communication beyond the ceilings of Article 54(4), the establishment of a monopoly of information, or the allocation of resources to media organisations by reference to the content published;
the suspension, postponement or cancellation of an election outside Article 123(4), the extension of the term of the Assembly by its own decision, or the alteration of an electoral district for a purpose prohibited by Article 187(5);
the removal, restriction or transfer of the jurisdiction of the Constitutional Court under Article 216(2), the shortening of a term protected by Article 223(3), or the exclusion of any act of public power from judicial review;
a state of emergency without a terminal date, its renewal without a fresh finding, or derogation from a right listed in Article 42(2);
indiscriminate collection, retention or analysis of the communications, movements or associations of the population, contrary to Article 50(3);
deprivation of citizenship, contrary to Article 58(4);
the registration, classification or scoring of persons or associations by opinion or by ideology, contrary to Articles 12(4), 51(7) and 53(5);
the conferral of a function named in Article 163(3) upon a body outside Article 163(1);
the contracting of external debt, the pledging of the common heritage or of a productive asset, or submission to external financial conditionality, contrary to Article 117(5);
the raising of an ecological ceiling otherwise than under Article 25(6), or the discharge of a domestic ceiling by an offset acquired outside the territory, contrary to Article 23(2);
compulsion to labour outside Article 48(3), and the conditioning of provision upon labour, contrary to Articles 48(2) and 70(4).
An act within paragraph 1 produces no legal effect, confers no right, and requires the obedience of no person.
Any person may plead the nullity before any court at any time, and Article 231(1) governs standing and costs.
Persons who proposed, adopted or executed an act within paragraph 1 are personally liable under Article 43(4), and Article 229(2) governs.
Title XII places the matters of this Article beyond the amending power.

An amendment may be proposed by:
one third of the members of the Assembly;
one hundred thousand residents, or three per cent of the residents registered, whichever is the lesser;
one third of the communal councils, representing together no less than one fifth of the residents of the Republic;
both regional councils acting jointly.
A proposal states the text to be inserted, amended or repealed, in the words proposed, and states the reasons.
Every proposal is published on deposit, together with its authors, its text and its reasons.
Signatures under paragraph 1(b) are collected within twelve months, are verifiable and revocable, and may be given in writing and through the public information system.
In no case is the exercise of an initiative under this Article conditioned upon the approval of any organ.

The following lie beyond the amending power, and in no case may they be amended, repealed, suspended, restricted or evaded by any procedure, by any majority, or by the replacement of this Constitution under Chapter 3:
human dignity, and its inviolability under Article 2;
the republican form, and the sovereignty of the people under Article 3;
the obligation to sustain the ecological conditions of life under Article 5(1), and the binding force of the ecological budgets under Article 26(1) and (2);
the social ownership of the means of production under Article 6(1) and (2), the abolition of income from the bare holding of productive assets under Article 6(3), and the prohibitions of Article 6(4);
the constitution of the Republic as a participatory democracy under Article 1(1), the universal, equal, free and secret suffrage of Article 60(1) and (2), and the periodic election of the Assembly;
the rights from which Article 42(2) permits no derogation;
the guarantee of essential provision under Article 62(2) and (3);
the equality of persons under Article 38(1) and (2), and the extension of the rights of Title III to every resident under Article 37(2);
the independence of the courts under Article 193, and the jurisdiction of the Constitutional Court under Article 216(2);
the acts declared void by Article 265, and the rule against attainment by indirection in Article 266;
this Article.
The core binds the amending power, the constituent power exercised under Chapter 3, the Assembly, the planning bodies, the territories, every majority and every referendum.
In no case does a referendum, however large its majority, authorise a measure touching the core, and Article 139(6) governs by analogy.
Provisions of Titles I, II and III outside the core are amended under Chapter 1 like any other provision of this Constitution.
